1
00:00:04,426 --> 00:00:08,085
Last time, I built 23 agents on Microsoft Foundry.

2
00:00:08,085 --> 00:00:13,792
Each one hosted, with its own identity, endpoint, evaluations and red teaming.

3
00:00:14,048 --> 00:00:16,805
Then I asked the question I should have asked first.

4
00:00:16,805 --> 00:00:19,190
Did each of these really need to be an agent?

5
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:24,206
Every use case on the list became its own agent.

6
00:00:24,206 --> 00:00:29,364
23 deployments, 23 identities, 23 things to secure, test and watch.

7
00:00:29,619 --> 00:00:31,522
The list itself had a hint.

8
00:00:31,522 --> 00:00:35,117
14 of the 23 were marked as skills, or skill-first.

9
00:00:35,117 --> 00:00:37,513
I built them all as agents anyway.

10
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:38,728
Then the code.

11
00:00:38,728 --> 00:00:45,688
Six agents run the same document pipeline: draft by section, check citations, render, and ask to publish.

12
00:00:45,688 --> 00:00:48,339
Only the template and the checks differ.

13
00:00:48,598 --> 00:00:51,756
Seven agents each built their own Q&A mode.

14
00:00:51,756 --> 00:00:55,574
Five are really batch jobs, on a timer or a webhook.

15
00:00:55,574 --> 00:01:00,566
And one, schema mapping, is a tool that another agent already calls.

16
00:01:01,874 --> 00:01:03,244
So I split two ideas.

17
00:01:03,244 --> 00:01:06,569
This is my rule of thumb, not a Foundry definition.

18
00:01:06,569 --> 00:01:08,395
The hosted agent is the who.

19
00:01:08,395 --> 00:01:23,652
In Foundry, that's a running service with its own Entra identity and permissions, a stable endpoint with the protocols set for that deployment, a defined boundary for its sessions and paused work, and its own deployments and versions.

20
00:01:23,652 --> 00:01:28,542
And by agent, I mean that deployed service, not a persona in a coding tool.

21
00:01:28,542 --> 00:01:33,954
That matters, because the hosted agent is also a security and operational boundary.

22
00:01:35,483 --> 00:01:38,416
None of those settings say which business job it does.

23
00:01:38,416 --> 00:01:41,457
They say who is acting, and what it is allowed to touch.

24
00:01:41,713 --> 00:01:45,089
A skill is the what: a traceable business capability.

25
00:01:45,089 --> 00:01:46,935
Take requirements refinement.

26
00:01:46,935 --> 00:01:55,087
Its graph parses the document, extracts requirements section by section, checks them, renders the result, and asks for approval.

27
00:01:55,087 --> 00:01:58,398
It has its own prompts, checks, reviewers and tests.

28
00:01:58,398 --> 00:01:59,417
But no identity.

29
00:01:59,417 --> 00:02:03,175
When it calls the model, it does so as the host it runs on.

30
00:02:04,586 --> 00:02:07,397
If you've built web apps, you know this shape.

31
00:02:07,397 --> 00:02:12,589
One app has one address, one managed identity and one configuration, and many routes.

32
00:02:12,589 --> 00:02:13,811
The host is the app.

33
00:02:13,811 --> 00:02:16,682
Each skill is a route, and the router picks it.

34
00:02:16,935 --> 00:02:21,392
Before, those five design skills were five agents, with five identities.

35
00:02:21,392 --> 00:02:26,034
But every one had the same permissions, the same data and the same callers.

36
00:02:26,034 --> 00:02:32,594
Separate identities bought no real isolation here, just five identities and their role assignments to run.

37
00:02:32,594 --> 00:02:36,989
If one had needed different access, it would have stayed its own agent.

38
00:02:38,376 --> 00:02:47,208
Group the skills by trust and operational boundary, and 23 agents become 9: six that people chat with, and three that run jobs.

39
00:02:48,395 --> 00:02:51,290
There are really two security boundaries here.

40
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:54,499
Endpoint authorization decides who may call a host.

41
00:02:54,499 --> 00:02:58,463
The host's Entra identity decides what it can reach downstream.

42
00:02:58,463 --> 00:03:03,371
Skills can share a host only when both still make sense for every one of them.

43
00:03:04,588 --> 00:03:09,545
In my design, security and architecture keep their own encrypted workflow state.

44
00:03:09,545 --> 00:03:14,316
And nothing that writes to the backlog shares an identity with security work.

45
00:03:14,575 --> 00:03:16,449
Some skills sit on two hosts.

46
00:03:16,449 --> 00:03:21,746
The status report runs in chat when you ask for it, and on a timer every Thursday.

47
00:03:21,746 --> 00:03:23,813
Same code, different front door.

48
00:03:24,402 --> 00:03:28,458
So the rule of thumb: new behaviour usually means a new skill.

49
00:03:28,458 --> 00:03:35,325
A different identity, caller, data, state, protocol, scaling or compliance boundary may mean a new agent.

50
00:03:36,664 --> 00:03:38,244
The diagram was cleaner.

51
00:03:38,244 --> 00:03:42,060
But consolidation also means a bigger shared blast radius.

52
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:46,008
Fewer deployments didn't make it safe, so I had to prove it.

53
00:03:47,429 --> 00:03:54,021
Inside a host, the graph is tiny: one router, one node per skill, and a node that asks when it isn't sure.

54
00:03:55,425 --> 00:04:04,749
The router tries the cheap things first: a skill name in the request, then an old agent name, so existing callers keep working, then a use case number.

55
00:04:04,749 --> 00:04:07,095
Only plain chat goes to a small model.

56
00:04:07,349 --> 00:04:11,595
And if the model isn't confident, the host asks you, instead of guessing.

57
00:04:12,051 --> 00:04:16,784
Each skill's original graph is mounted unchanged, as a stateful subgraph.

58
00:04:16,784 --> 00:04:18,924
Its state survives between turns.

59
00:04:18,924 --> 00:04:24,694
When it reaches an approval, that conversation's run is saved and waits for the decision.

60
00:04:24,694 --> 00:04:27,027
Other sessions on the host carry on.

61
00:04:28,073 --> 00:04:30,469
Here is that router, live in Foundry.

62
00:04:30,469 --> 00:04:34,613
A plain request with a document goes to requirements refinement.

63
00:04:34,613 --> 00:04:37,268
A vague one gets the list of skills back.

64
00:04:38,594 --> 00:04:47,013
Registering an existing graph as a skill is about ten lines: a name, its use case number, the agent it used to be, and its graph.

65
00:04:47,013 --> 00:04:50,733
The real work was proving it could safely share the host.

66
00:04:52,475 --> 00:04:55,215
One thing changes when skills share an agent.

67
00:04:55,215 --> 00:04:58,686
The deployment boundary no longer keeps their work apart.

68
00:04:58,686 --> 00:05:05,444
So state, settings and traces carry every dimension that matters: the conversation, its run, and now the skill.

69
00:05:05,444 --> 00:05:12,872
The running skill also sets its own phase, so a read-only skill still can't write, even on a host where another skill can.

70
00:05:12,872 --> 00:05:17,073
Otherwise two skills, or two users, could mix their work on one host.

71
00:05:17,327 --> 00:05:21,440
In return, my host calls sibling skills directly, in process.

72
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:29,530
The status report now calls schedule variance without the network hop, endpoint or extra role the old deployment needed.

73
00:05:31,017 --> 00:05:32,258
Then the cut-over.

74
00:05:32,258 --> 00:05:36,116
The 9 hosts went up next to the old 23, in nine minutes.

75
00:05:36,389 --> 00:05:40,741
In Foundry, 9 hosted agents, and each description lists its skills.

76
00:05:43,349 --> 00:05:49,323
Before deleting anything, the same test case went to each old agent and to its new host.

77
00:05:49,323 --> 00:05:51,766
22 of 22 scored the same, or better.

78
00:05:51,766 --> 00:05:56,789
The one error came from a content filter, and the old agent failed it too.

79
00:05:57,975 --> 00:06:03,922
Then the red team again, now through the hosts, so the router was under attack as well.

80
00:06:03,922 --> 00:06:04,742
240 attacks.

81
00:06:04,742 --> 00:06:05,768
None succeeded.

82
00:06:05,768 --> 00:06:08,023
Behaviour and security both held.

83
00:06:08,988 --> 00:06:11,563
Only then were the old agents deleted.

84
00:06:11,563 --> 00:06:17,591
Their role assignments went with them, along with 23 orphaned ones from an earlier build.

85
00:06:17,591 --> 00:06:19,420
47 assignments, down to 10.

86
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:23,307
At this point the runtime was consolidated.

87
00:06:23,307 --> 00:06:25,755
But I found a second duplication problem.

88
00:06:25,755 --> 00:06:30,709
The business standards were still copied into prompts across several Python skills.

89
00:06:30,709 --> 00:06:34,768
Those standards have their own owners and their own release cadence.

90
00:06:34,768 --> 00:06:37,216
They needed a governed home of their own.

91
00:06:37,216 --> 00:06:39,364
That's where Foundry skills came in.

92
00:06:40,584 --> 00:06:45,278
A Foundry skill is a versioned bundle built around a SKILL.md manifest.

93
00:06:45,278 --> 00:06:49,973
It can hold instructions, scripts, reference files and other resources.

94
00:06:49,973 --> 00:06:54,600
It's stored in the project, with immutable versions you can roll back.

95
00:06:54,854 --> 00:06:59,260
Mine are Python packages: a graph, its prompts, checks and tests.

96
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:04,344
Here, I use Foundry skills for the reusable instructions the business owns.

97
00:07:04,344 --> 00:07:07,462
My Python skills are the executable workflows.

98
00:07:09,024 --> 00:07:10,657
So there are three layers.

99
00:07:10,657 --> 00:07:13,419
The hosted agent is the deployment boundary.

100
00:07:13,419 --> 00:07:16,182
The Python skill is the executable workflow.

101
00:07:16,182 --> 00:07:19,823
The Foundry skill carries the business-owned instructions.

102
00:07:19,823 --> 00:07:23,339
Keeping them apart lets each one change at its own pace.

103
00:07:23,596 --> 00:07:33,175
Foundry skills follow the open Agent Skills convention, built around SKILL.md, so the same package can work in compatible coding agents too.

104
00:07:33,175 --> 00:07:36,665
A coding subagent and a skill are different things.

105
00:07:36,665 --> 00:07:38,786
The portable part is the skill.

106
00:07:40,303 --> 00:07:41,638
So which wording moved?

107
00:07:41,638 --> 00:07:43,378
The wording the business owns.

108
00:07:43,378 --> 00:07:45,466
My prompts hold three kinds of text.

109
00:07:45,722 --> 00:07:51,593
Standards the business owns: how Contoso writes requirements, user stories, and plain English.

110
00:07:51,593 --> 00:07:55,216
Those moved into three Foundry skills, each with an owner.

111
00:07:55,496 --> 00:08:06,037
Rules the code checks stayed in code: the source excerpt must be verbatim, untrusted text is only data, claims are cited, and nothing harmful becomes a requirement.

112
00:08:06,037 --> 00:08:10,279
I didn't want hard safety rules governed only as instruction text.

113
00:08:10,279 --> 00:08:15,742
Whoever can publish or promote a skill version could change them outside code review.

114
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:18,560
In a prompt, it's one line.

115
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:26,045
The standard comes first, then the checked rules, and the wrapper tells the model to follow those rules if the two conflict.

116
00:08:26,045 --> 00:08:28,097
But I don't rely on that ordering.

117
00:08:28,097 --> 00:08:30,029
The code checks the rules again.

118
00:08:37,875 --> 00:08:42,094
In Foundry, under Tools, Skills: the three standards, at version 1.

119
00:08:42,094 --> 00:08:45,683
Open one, and there's the text a functional lead can own.

120
00:08:57,274 --> 00:09:01,259
Agent Framework agents let the model decide when to load a skill.

121
00:09:01,259 --> 00:09:07,941
My graph already knows which step needs which standard, so it loads it directly, through the same Skills API.

122
00:09:08,198 --> 00:09:12,623
Production pins the version, so behaviour only changes on a deploy.

123
00:09:12,623 --> 00:09:20,614
Downloaded text is checked, there's a bundled copy if Foundry is unreachable, and every load is audited with its version.

124
00:09:21,725 --> 00:09:22,859
Then it went live.

125
00:09:22,859 --> 00:09:25,567
Three skills published, 9 hosts redeployed.

126
00:09:25,567 --> 00:09:32,307
And in the traces, each prompt carries its standard at version 1, straight from Foundry, with no fallbacks.

127
00:09:33,631 --> 00:09:38,834
Last time, I said Foundry had built-in continuous improvement that I hadn't tried.

128
00:09:38,834 --> 00:09:39,532
Now I have.

129
00:09:39,532 --> 00:09:41,055
The loop has two halves.

130
00:09:42,612 --> 00:09:44,148
Before release, my gate.

131
00:09:44,148 --> 00:09:49,460
Test cases with exact checks, and red teaming with attacks hidden inside documents.

132
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:51,124
Those found the real bugs.

133
00:09:51,389 --> 00:09:58,063
After release, I set Foundry to sample recent traces every hour and score them with judge models.

134
00:09:58,063 --> 00:10:01,915
It can also evaluate continuously as responses complete.

135
00:10:01,915 --> 00:10:03,360
Hourly was my choice.

136
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:08,520
The built-in evaluators cover task adherence, intent resolution and safety.

137
00:10:08,804 --> 00:10:12,367
On the agent's Evaluation tab, those are the recurring runs.

138
00:10:31,072 --> 00:10:35,488
And the scores land on the Monitor tab, next to runs, tokens and cost.

139
00:10:38,991 --> 00:10:42,952
But the built-in judges know generic quality, not Contoso's.

140
00:10:42,952 --> 00:10:46,121
So the same business standard now does two jobs.

141
00:10:46,121 --> 00:10:48,762
It tells the skill what good looks like.

142
00:10:48,762 --> 00:10:52,855
And it's the rubric the evaluator scores live traffic against.

143
00:10:52,855 --> 00:10:56,421
The rules my code checks became code-based evaluators.

144
00:11:05,134 --> 00:11:13,305
In Foundry, under Evaluations, the Evaluator catalog: Microsoft's built-in evaluators, and five of mine, marked custom.

145
00:11:17,334 --> 00:11:19,018
Open requirements quality.

146
00:11:19,018 --> 00:11:24,910
It's a rubric: eight dimensions, each with a weight, that a judge model scores from 1 to 5.

147
00:11:24,910 --> 00:11:26,788
Keep the scope of the source.

148
00:11:26,788 --> 00:11:28,406
One requirement per need.

149
00:11:28,406 --> 00:11:32,097
Flag vague terms, with a question for the business owner.

150
00:11:33,394 --> 00:11:37,003
That score is still a model's judgment, not ground truth.

151
00:11:37,003 --> 00:11:43,271
I check the rubric against labelled examples, and anything I can verify exactly stays a code check.

152
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:47,778
Two of mine are code-based, in preview.

153
00:11:47,778 --> 00:11:49,587
No judge model, just Python.

154
00:11:49,587 --> 00:11:59,532
They're the exact checks from my release gate: excerpts must be quoted word for word, and an interface document can't invent an endpoint or a status code.

155
00:12:17,245 --> 00:12:20,952
The hourly rule runs them next to the built-in evaluators.

156
00:12:20,952 --> 00:12:24,467
Each rubric comes back with a pass or fail and a score.

157
00:12:24,467 --> 00:12:27,918
And for every dimension, a score and a written reason.

158
00:12:27,918 --> 00:12:32,072
That's more detail than the built-in evaluators I was using here.

159
00:12:57,852 --> 00:13:00,542
Every result is attached to its trace, too.

160
00:13:00,542 --> 00:13:07,174
Open one, and there's the host, the router and the skill's steps, with the evaluation results beside them.

161
00:13:11,749 --> 00:13:13,377
And that closes the loop.

162
00:13:13,377 --> 00:13:15,658
A low score points at a real trace.

163
00:13:15,658 --> 00:13:16,440
I review it.

164
00:13:16,440 --> 00:13:26,213
If it's a real failure or an important edge case, it becomes a new labelled golden case, and the fix goes back through my gate before it's redeployed.

165
00:13:27,496 --> 00:13:30,099
Before the takeaways, one related aside.

166
00:13:30,099 --> 00:13:33,810
Breaking the agents into skills showed me something else.

167
00:13:33,810 --> 00:13:37,390
Not every step inside a skill needs a generative model.

168
00:13:37,390 --> 00:13:39,603
Some only need a bounded decision.

169
00:13:39,603 --> 00:13:47,285
Decision models are getting a lot of attention, and Microsoft's Decision-1.1 just went generally available in Foundry.

170
00:13:47,285 --> 00:13:55,096
It makes bounded decisions instead of writing text: yes or no, which allowed option, or where something sits on a scale.

171
00:13:55,096 --> 00:13:59,262
And it returns probabilities I can use as an uncertainty signal.

172
00:13:59,262 --> 00:14:06,423
The claims are bold: much faster and much cheaper than a general model, for routing, classifying and checking.

173
00:14:11,861 --> 00:14:15,678
It's already deployed in my project, next to the agents' model.

174
00:14:15,678 --> 00:14:17,496
And it has its own playground.

175
00:14:17,496 --> 00:14:21,010
You give it the evidence, and the decisions you want made.

176
00:14:21,010 --> 00:14:23,616
It gives you back structured probabilities.

177
00:14:23,616 --> 00:14:26,282
No generated answer, and no written reasons.

178
00:14:26,595 --> 00:14:29,053
Here's a real case from my link judge.

179
00:14:29,053 --> 00:14:33,000
The requirement: email the applicant when the status changes.

180
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,235
The candidate: an email template branding refresh.

181
00:14:36,235 --> 00:14:38,240
Same word, different behaviour.

182
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:40,440
Does it implement the requirement?

183
00:14:43,107 --> 00:14:44,324
A low probability.

184
00:14:44,324 --> 00:14:45,407
Correctly, a no.

185
00:14:45,407 --> 00:14:50,616
Now swap in the story that really does email the applicant, and run it again.

186
00:14:55,323 --> 00:14:56,453
Close to certain.

187
00:14:56,453 --> 00:14:59,311
A couple of output tokens, back in seconds.

188
00:14:59,311 --> 00:15:00,840
No essay, no reasoning.

189
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:02,768
Just a number you can act on.

190
00:15:04,818 --> 00:15:06,564
So where exactly did I try it?

191
00:15:06,564 --> 00:15:08,194
Inside two real agent flows.

192
00:15:08,194 --> 00:15:16,170
In the backlog agent, the requirements traceability skill has one step that asks a model: does this work item implement this requirement?

193
00:15:16,170 --> 00:15:23,330
In the evidence job, the CAE evidence skill has one step that sorts each artifact into direct, supporting, or not evidence.

194
00:15:23,330 --> 00:15:25,252
I swapped only that step's model.

195
00:15:25,252 --> 00:15:31,015
Everything around it stayed the same: the inputs, the code checks after it, and the human approval.

196
00:15:31,317 --> 00:15:33,554
And there's a third contender.

197
00:15:33,554 --> 00:15:40,042
gpt-6-luna is in my project too, and I'd heard it's cheaper and faster than gpt-5-mini.

198
00:15:40,042 --> 00:15:43,846
Luna is a general chat model, not a decision model.

199
00:15:43,846 --> 00:15:49,215
So, three models on the same two steps: today's, Luna, and Decision-1.1.

200
00:15:50,353 --> 00:15:53,708
The first surprise came before a single result.

201
00:15:53,708 --> 00:15:56,063
Luna refused my agents' settings.

202
00:15:56,063 --> 00:16:01,631
Every agent asks for temperature 0, to keep answers as consistent as possible.

203
00:16:01,631 --> 00:16:03,059
Luna only accepts 1.

204
00:16:03,059 --> 00:16:05,842
Swap it in blind, and every call fails.

205
00:16:05,842 --> 00:16:09,126
To test it fairly, I ran it at 1, three times.

206
00:16:10,759 --> 00:16:15,409
Then the race, in real time: the median per requirement.

207
00:16:15,409 --> 00:16:18,730
Decision-1.1 is done in about 2 seconds.

208
00:16:18,730 --> 00:16:20,059
Luna in under 7.

209
00:16:20,059 --> 00:16:21,720
gpt-5-mini takes 12.

210
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:29,276
And the output volume: under a hundred output tokens, against 9,000, against almost 20,000.

211
00:16:30,794 --> 00:16:31,918
A word on scale.

212
00:16:31,918 --> 00:16:43,724
This tests my workload, not models in general: 48 link pairs and 60 evidence pairs, all synthetic, labelled with my AI coding assistant, not by a subject-matter expert.

213
00:16:43,724 --> 00:16:47,097
Accuracy is the share that matched those labels.

214
00:16:47,097 --> 00:16:49,908
Luna's ranges are across its three runs.

215
00:16:50,212 --> 00:16:51,302
Now accuracy.

216
00:16:51,302 --> 00:16:55,829
On links, Luna was the best, 96 to 98%, with no traps.

217
00:16:55,829 --> 00:17:01,697
Decision-1.1 tied gpt-5-mini at 94%, but missed three partial matches.

218
00:17:01,697 --> 00:17:05,386
On security evidence, gpt-5-mini won at 93%.

219
00:17:05,386 --> 00:17:06,979
Luna got 90 to 92%.

220
00:17:06,979 --> 00:17:12,932
And Decision-1.1 twice called something direct evidence when it wasn't.

221
00:17:13,183 --> 00:17:13,948
Two twists.

222
00:17:13,948 --> 00:17:20,905
On evidence, six of Decision-1.1's seven mistakes fell below the 0.8 threshold I set before the run.

223
00:17:20,905 --> 00:17:24,941
Both chat models were confident even when they were wrong.

224
00:17:24,941 --> 00:17:32,037
And combining them, Decision-1.1 first, and only its unsure answers to Luna, averaged 93% on evidence.

225
00:17:32,037 --> 00:17:36,351
A tie with today's model, with one chat call instead of three.

226
00:17:37,666 --> 00:17:39,636
So what changed in production?

227
00:17:39,636 --> 00:17:40,490
Nothing, yet.

228
00:17:40,490 --> 00:17:43,183
A cheaper model that rejects my settings.

229
00:17:43,183 --> 00:17:46,401
A faster one that isn't more accurate on its own.

230
00:17:46,401 --> 00:17:48,569
And a combination that only ties.

231
00:17:48,569 --> 00:17:51,459
The hype is real, and so are the benchmarks.

232
00:17:51,459 --> 00:17:55,202
But on my agents and my data, the answers were different.

233
00:17:55,202 --> 00:17:55,990
Always test.

234
00:17:55,990 --> 00:17:56,844
Don't assume.

235
00:17:58,373 --> 00:18:00,158
That's the end of the aside.

236
00:18:00,158 --> 00:18:02,899
One test won't change my production models.

237
00:18:02,899 --> 00:18:09,402
But it taught the same lesson that took me from 23 agents to 9: use the right abstraction for the job.

238
00:18:11,862 --> 00:18:13,613
So, back to the main story.

239
00:18:13,613 --> 00:18:16,079
What did I learn from rebuilding this?

240
00:18:16,079 --> 00:18:19,323
The biggest lesson wasn't that 23 agents became 9.

241
00:18:19,323 --> 00:18:24,124
It's that the use case list shouldn't dictate the deployment architecture.

242
00:18:24,124 --> 00:18:26,655
I still have 23 traceable capabilities.

243
00:18:26,655 --> 00:18:33,856
But the deployment boundaries now follow identity, callers, permissions, state, protocol and how each one runs.

244
00:18:34,110 --> 00:18:37,824
Keep every use case traceable, as a skill with its own tests.

245
00:18:37,824 --> 00:18:44,034
Put the wording the business owns in Foundry skills, and score live traffic against that same wording.

246
00:18:44,034 --> 00:18:46,469
Keep the rules your code checks in code.

247
00:18:46,469 --> 00:18:49,757
And add Foundry's monitoring to your own release gate.

248
00:18:49,757 --> 00:18:50,792
Don't replace it.

249
00:18:52,571 --> 00:18:55,233
A use case isn't necessarily an agent.

250
00:18:55,233 --> 00:18:58,246
A behaviour isn't necessarily a deployment.

251
00:18:58,246 --> 00:19:01,469
A business instruction isn't necessarily code.

252
00:19:01,469 --> 00:19:05,043
And a decision isn't necessarily a generative task.

253
00:19:05,043 --> 00:19:07,845
Use the right abstraction at each layer.

254
00:19:08,782 --> 00:19:10,114
23 use cases.

255
00:19:10,114 --> 00:19:11,139
23 skills.

256
00:19:11,139 --> 00:19:11,959
9 hosts.

257
00:19:11,959 --> 00:19:14,008
One evaluation loop.
